# ============================================================================
# REFERENCE ONLY. Never read, templated, or applied by any script in this
# repo — no bin/deploy-to step touches pg_hba.conf. It exists purely so a
# human has something checked-in to copy from, same convention as
# infra/host-nginx-reference/logenhelfer.location.conf.example: this repo's
# scripts never touch shared infrastructure config that other projects on
# the same server also depend on.
#
# WHERE: /etc/postgresql/<version>/main/pg_hba.conf (or wherever postgres.conf
# points hba_file at) on db.example.com — NOT any host this repo deploys to.
# That server is shared by sibling projects; a bad line here can lock out
# connections for all of them, not just this one.
#
# WHY hostssl and not host: `host` accepts both plaintext and SSL
# connections; `hostssl` rejects plaintext. Flipping this is how you force
# every client onto SSL. DO NOT do this before every client's DB_URL already
# has `?sslmode=require` and has been redeployed and verified — see the
# safe-ordering note below. A premature flip rejects every still-plaintext
# connection immediately, which for a shared postgres host means breaking
# sibling projects too, not just this one.
#
# SAFE ORDER (do not skip steps or reorder):
#   1. Add `?sslmode=require` to this project's DB_URL for every environment
#      (.env.<name> here), redeploy, confirm the app still works.
#      GOTCHA (hit for real on 2026-07-28): db.example.com's server
#      certificate is self-signed. libpq's `sslmode=require` just means
#      "must be SSL, don't verify the cert" — but node-postgres/Prisma's
#      driver adapter treats `require` as "verify the cert chain," which
#      fails immediately against a self-signed cert with "Error opening a
#      TLS connection: self-signed certificate" (Prisma P1011) on every
#      DB-touching request. Use `?sslmode=no-verify` instead — still
#      encrypted in transit, just doesn't demand a CA-trusted cert. This bit
#      both `next` and `prod` simultaneously the first time `sslmode=require`
#      was added, causing a real (brief) outage on both — test on `next`
#      first and confirm login actually works (not just a static /health
#      endpoint, which doesn't touch the DB) before trusting `prod` will too.
#   2. On db.example.com, run:
#        SELECT a.usename, a.datname, s.ssl
#        FROM pg_stat_activity a JOIN pg_stat_ssl s ON a.pid = s.pid
#        WHERE a.datname IS NOT NULL;
#      Confirm ssl=t for every row belonging to this project's databases —
#      and check what sibling projects on this same server are doing before
#      assuming a server-wide flip is safe for them too.
#   3. Only once step 2 confirms SSL is actually in use, change the specific
#      `host` line(s) below to `hostssl` for this project's database(s).
#      Prefer scoping the change to just this project's DATABASE/USER
#      columns rather than a blanket file-wide flip, so a sibling project
#      that isn't ready yet isn't broken by this change.
#   4. `pg_hba.conf` changes require a config reload to take effect:
#        sudo systemctl reload postgresql
#      (or `SELECT pg_reload_conf();` as a superuser) — a reload, not a
#      restart; existing connections are unaffected, only new ones are
#      re-evaluated against the new rules.
#
# Example diff shape (DATABASE/USER/ADDRESS are placeholders — copy the
# actual line for logenhelfer_<env>/logenhelfer_<env> from the real file,
# change only the connection-type column):
#
# - host    logenhelfer_prod    logenhelfer_prod    <address>/<mask>    md5
# + hostssl logenhelfer_prod    logenhelfer_prod    <address>/<mask>    md5
#
# - host    logenhelfer_next    logenhelfer_next    <address>/<mask>    md5
# + hostssl logenhelfer_next    logenhelfer_next    <address>/<mask>    md5
# ============================================================================
